    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d352">

                <pb facs="0341" n="331" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg331"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P.W./594: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-10-31T18:00:00+09:00">October 31, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 31—12:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1722. Continuing Embassy’s telegram No. 1721, October 31, 5 p.m.<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d352-f1"/> In summing up his interview with the Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs,<anchor n="20" xml:id="d352-f2"/> the British Ambassador reported to
                    the Foreign Office that he believed that, while the Minister was genuinely
                    anxious to bring about an improvement in relations with Great Britain, he was
                    chiefly preoccupied at the present time with the situation in Southeastern Asia
                    concerning which he is probably under strong pressure and that, therefore, the
                    time left for effecting a solution of relations with Japan may be shorter than
                    the Ambassador had previously anticipated.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
